 "caution": "1.8 million words, versus hundreds of millions for the main languages. Frequency values are unreliable, so there are no frequency bands, only the all-words generators. The reference lexicon has 24,062 words, so generators, neighbourhood measures and cluster filters are rougher than for the main languages. 24,062 of the reference words come from a Wikipedia corpus (Leipzig Corpora Collection, bar_wikipedia_2021_100K, 1.8 million words); no subtitle list exists for this language. Written sources are more formal than subtitles and contain more names and foreign words (71 probable English words were removed). All frequencies come from that corpus, so no frequency bands are offered. Bavarian is a regional variety close to German. Every word form of German (821,524 forms) was added to the reject list, so no pseudoword is a real German word, and the app pre-selects German under \"Exclude real words of other languages\" (the Word-in column shows near misses). Of the 24,062 reference words, 3,700 are also German words; they stay in the lexicon because they are words of the variety too. Bavarian spelling is not standardised (the Wikipedia follows several conventions); å marks the dark a.",
